  "prompt_spec": {
    "original_prompt": "From Aristotle's works, it is clear that phantasia is causally downstream from aisthesis ontologically: phantasia ontologically depends on aisthesis but is different from it. Articulate the ontological chain of causal dependency, as aristotle understands it, of the following (each \"-->\" illustrates the following item being causally downstream): motion-->time-->aisthesis-->phantasia-->resonant aisthema and pathos -->phantasmata-->thinking, recollection, imagination, etc.",
    "research_questions": [
      "What is the ontological chain of causal dependency in Aristotle's philosophy from motion through time, aisthesis, phantasia, resonant aisthema and pathos, phantasmata, to higher cognitive functions like thinking, recollection, and imagination?"
    ],
    "required_facets": [
      {
        "facet_id": "ad371b53-15ce-4d48-ab33-e1414a0397aa",
        "name": "Motion and Time as Foundational Conditions",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish motion (kinesis) as the ontological foundation for time (chronos) in Physics Book IV, and how does this temporal framework provide the necessary conditions for perceptual experience? This facet investigates Aristotle's argument that time is the measure or number of motion with respect to before and after, examining passages from Physics where he establishes that without motion there can be no time, and exploring how this metaphysical priority creates the framework within which aisthesis becomes possible as a temporally extended process of receiving sensible forms.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "3fc68721-c0dd-42af-9402-93ed8cf4b7fb",
        "name": "Aisthesis as Dependent on Temporal Motion",
        "description": "What is the causal relationship between time and sense-perception (aisthesis) in Aristotle's De Anima and how does aisthesis require the temporal framework established by motion? This facet examines Aristotle's theory that aisthesis involves the reception of sensible forms without matter, investigating passages from De Anima Book II and III where perception is described as a process of actualization that necessarily occurs in time, exploring how the sense organs undergo qualitative change (alloiosis) through contact with sensible objects, and analyzing why perceptual discrimination requires temporal extension and cannot occur in an instantaneous moment.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "70adf603-9fe4-4e53-87ae-2a76c1272b87",
        "name": "Phantasia's Ontological Dependence on Aisthesis",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish in De Anima Book III chapter 3 that phantasia (imagination or appearance) is causally downstream from and ontologically dependent upon aisthesis while remaining distinct from it? This facet investigates Aristotle's explicit statements that phantasia cannot occur without prior sense-perception, examining his argument that phantasia is a movement produced by actual sensation (kinesis apo tes aistheseos tes kat' energeian), analyzing passages where he distinguishes phantasia from both perception and thought, and exploring how phantasia preserves and processes sensory content after the sensible object is no longer present, thus requiring aisthesis as its necessary causal antecedent.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "2618415d-073b-45d0-978b-4a37ad381aac",
        "name": "Resonant Aisthema and Pathos as Intermediary States",
        "description": "What role do resonant aisthema (sense-impressions or affections of the sense organs) and pathos (affective states or passive modifications) play as intermediary stages between aisthesis and phantasmata in Aristotle's psychology, particularly in De Anima, De Memoria, and De Insomniis? This facet examines how aisthesis produces lasting aisthemata that persist in the sense organs and soul, investigating Aristotle's account of how perceptual experiences generate pathē (affections or emotions) that accompany and modify sensory impressions, analyzing passages where he describes the physiological and psychological residues of perception, and exploring how these resonant states provide the material basis from which phantasmata are subsequently formed.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "5be71139-ab83-4ed0-84c0-4f3e822d0610",
        "name": "Phantasmata as Products of Phantasia",
        "description": "How does Aristotle characterize phantasmata (mental images or imaginative representations) as the products generated by phantasia from resonant aisthemata and pathē, and what is their functional role in De Anima Book III, De Memoria et Reminiscentia, and De Insomniis? This facet investigates Aristotle's account of how phantasmata are formed from the processing of sensory residues, examining passages where he describes phantasmata as quasi-pictorial representations that can be manipulated independently of current perception, analyzing his claim that phantasmata are like aisthemata except that they lack the matter, and exploring how these mental images serve as the necessary substrate for higher cognitive operations.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "8920f718-8cd3-48ee-93f5-6fb7794f405e",
        "name": "Higher Cognitive Functions Dependent on Phantasmata",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish that thinking (noēsis), recollection (anamnēsis), and deliberate imagination depend causally on phantasmata as their necessary cognitive substrate in De Anima Book III chapters 7-8, De Memoria et Reminiscentia, and Metaphysics? This facet examines Aristotle's famous claim that the soul never thinks without phantasmata (oudepote noei aneu phantasmatos hē psychē), investigating passages where he argues that intellectual cognition requires mental images as its material basis, analyzing his account of how recollection involves the deliberate retrieval and sequencing of phantasmata, exploring how practical deliberation (bouleusis) operates through imaginative representation of possible actions, and examining how even abstract mathematical thinking depends on visualizable phantasmata.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      }
    ],
